---
/**
 * `/how-it-works` - the equipment drawing and the long read.
 *
 * Written for an engineer who wants to argue with it, which means the numbers have to be the real
 * ones and the limits have to be stated by us rather than found by them. The section on what the
 * guarantee does NOT cover is the most important on the page.
 */
import { DEFAULT_POLICY, MS_PER_DAY } from '@throughline/memory';
import Board from '../layouts/Board.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../scripts/schema.ts';
import type { SourceRow } from '../scripts/types.ts';

const site = Astro.site?.href ?? Astro.url.origin;
const policy = DEFAULT_POLICY;
const graceHours = policy.graceWindowMs / (MS_PER_DAY / 24);

const sources: readonly SourceRow[] = [
  { claim: 'Every policy number below, imported from packages/memory/src/policy.ts', trust: 'MEASURED' },
  { claim: 'That vector indexing works on the free Basic tier, measured against a live cluster', trust: 'MEASURED' },
  { claim: 'The Mackay citation, checked against its DOI', trust: 'MEASURED' },
];
---

<Board
  title="How it works - Throughline"
  heading="How Throughline makes agent recall auditable"
  description="The schema, the recall pipeline, the coverage verdict, the capability probe and the eviction policy, with the limits of the guarantee stated rather than implied."
  designator={['TLN-01 / Equipment drawing', 'Recall pipeline', 'Coverage verdicts']}
  sources={sources}
  jsonLd={articleSchema(site, '/how-it-works/', 'How Throughline makes agent recall auditable')}
>
  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="pipeline">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Section 1</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="pipeline">The recall pipeline</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A recall runs in a fixed order, and every stage that can fail reports which stage it was.
        The path is chosen first, from what a capability probe actually observed about the cluster.
        Then the query is embedded. Then candidates are read, scored in code, and filtered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The order matters for a reason that is easy to get backwards: because BOTH retrieval paths
        order by vector distance, both need a query vector. An embedder outage is therefore total.
        There is no "force an exact scan" control on this site, because that control cannot exist -
        the exact scan needs the embedder too. Copy offering one would be describing a product we
        did not build.
      </p>

      <h3>The verdict</h3>
      <p>
        Every recall returns a receipt carrying the query, which path ran, how many candidates were
        examined, what was excluded and under which rule, the elapsed time, and one of three
        verdicts.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>COVERED</strong> - the search ran over everything it was meant to. An empty result here is a real absence.</li>
        <li><strong>PARTIAL</strong> - it was cut short by the candidate cap of {policy.candidateCap}. What came back is real and incomplete.</li>
        <li><strong>UNKNOWN</strong> - it could not run. An empty result here says nothing whatsoever, and the agent may not treat it as an absence.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A failure outranks a cap. A search that broke halfway through a truncated candidate set is
        UNKNOWN, not PARTIAL, because downgrading in the other direction would let a broken search
        present itself as merely incomplete.
      </p>

      <h3>Three controls, because a prompt is a suggestion</h3>
      <p>
        The agent is structurally prevented from reporting an absence it did not establish. First,
        the tool result for a failed recall leads with the failure, so there is no reading of it in
        which the model sees memories before it sees that the search did not run. Second, the loop
        tracks the worst verdict any recall returned this turn, which is state the model cannot
        influence. Third, the final answer is checked against that verdict, and an answer asserting
        absence when coverage was not COVERED is refused and the model is told why.
      </p>

      <h3>What the guarantee does not cover</h3>
      <p>
        The guarantee is per turn and per phrasing, not per question. The loop records that SOME
        recall this turn came back COVERED; nothing binds an absence claim to the SUBJECT that was
        searched. A turn that recalls "checkout latency", gets a COVERED empty result, and then
        asserts that nothing is on record about the payments database is permitted. That is a real
        hole, not a quibble, and closing it needs the claim's subject matched against the queries
        actually run - a semantic judgement this loop deliberately does not make, because a model
        deciding whether its own sentence is about the thing it searched is the same theatre as a
        model deciding when to audit itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the guarantee as: an absence claim cannot survive a turn in which no search completed.
        It can survive a turn that searched something else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="decay">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Section 2</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="decay">Staleness is computed, not guessed</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="rack">
      {([
        ['entity_fact', 'ENTITY FACT', '"The primary is db-7" is true on Tuesday and dangerous on Friday'],
        ['observation', 'OBSERVATION', 'A single reading. It ages, but it was true when recorded'],
        ['runbook_fact', 'RUNBOOK FACT', 'Durable, right up until a migration quietly invalidates it'],
        ['resolution', 'RESOLUTION', 'What actually fixed something'],
        ['rejected_hypothesis', 'REJECTED HYPOTHESIS', 'Stays true more or less forever, and it is the cheapest thing an on-call engineer can be told'],
      ] as const).map(([kind, label, why]) => (
        <div class="strip">
          <span class="holder"></span>
          <div>
            <div class="row r-three">
              <div class="cell"><b>Kind</b><span class="val">{label}</span></div>
              <div class="cell"><b>Half-life</b><span class="val">{policy.halfLifeDays[kind]} d</span></div>
              <div class="cell"><b>Why</b><span class="say">{why}</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Below a freshness of {policy.staleFloor}, meaning older than one half-life for its kind, a
        memory is returned FLAGGED rather than dropped. Hiding it would remove the human's ability
        to notice that the system is running on old information. On this site that flag is a strip
        cocked out of alignment, which is what a controller did to a paper strip that needed
        attention.
      </p>
      <p>
        The similarity floor is {policy.similarityFloor}, in the remapped space where orthogonal
        vectors land on 0.5 rather than 0. That detail is load bearing: a floor below 0.5 would
        exclude nothing at all while its exclusion count sat at zero forever, looking like a
        working filter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="cockroach">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Section 3</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="cockroach">What the database is doing</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        CockroachDB is the system of record. Semantic recall runs over a <code>VECTOR</code> column
        with the cosine operator class, and the index is treated as an accelerator, never as a
        correctness dependency: if it is unavailable, recall falls back to an exact scan and says so
        in the receipt and on the status page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things worth knowing, both measured against a live cluster rather than taken from
        documentation. Vector indexing works on the free Basic tier, which is not documented
        anywhere we could find. And a vector index on the embedding column alone is useless here:
        CockroachDB accelerates a filtered nearest-neighbour query only when the filters match the
        index's PREFIX columns, so an unprefixed index plans as a full scan for every query this
        system runs. The index is <code>(workspace_id, is_live, embedding vector_cosine_ops)</code>,
        where <code>is_live</code> is a stored computed column so it cannot drift from the tombstone
        it is derived from.
      </p>
      <p>
        The capability probe confirms the planner really chooses that index by reading the query
        plan, not by checking that an index exists. An index the planner ignores accelerates
        nothing, and reporting a catalog row as a capability is how a system ends up claiming
        something it does not have.
      </p>
      <p>
        Eviction is a transaction that writes a tombstone, and nothing inside the {graceHours} hour
        grace window can be evicted at any score.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="anchor">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Section 4</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="anchor">Why a strip board</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="rack">
      <div class="strip">
        <span class="holder h-run"></span>
        <div>
          <div class="row r-main">
            <div class="cell">
              <b>Cited</b>
              <span class="say">
                Controllers refused to give up paper flight strips because the physical record was
                doing safety work no screen replicated.
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="cell"><b>Kind</b><span class="val">RUNBOOK FACT</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="row r-three">
            <div class="cell"><b>Source</b><span class="val">Mackay, ACM TOCHI 1999</span></div>
            <div class="cell"><b>DOI</b><span class="val">10.1145/331490.331491</span></div>
            <div class="cell"><b>Asserted by</b><span class="val">human:design</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Before radar data blocks, every aircraft in a sector was a strip of card in a coloured
        holder, racked on a painted metal board. The strip existed to keep a legal record of the
        instructions actually issued, written in real time, under pressure, by people who could not
        afford to trust their own recall. That is the product, not a mood borrowed from it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The obvious aviation picks, which this deliberately is not: the black box, the accident
        report, the evidence room. All three are records of the autopsy. The strip board is the
        record made DURING, which is where on-call actually lives at 3am.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Board>
